---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SpeakerPlanner from '../../components/SpeakerPlanner.tsx';

const title = 'Dolby Atmos speaker planner';
const description =
  'Plan Dolby Atmos speakers from 5.1 to 9.1.6: automatic placement in your room, azimuth and elevation angles from the main listening position, checked against Dolby’s installation guidelines.';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'At what angle should Atmos ceiling speakers be mounted?',
    a: 'Seen from your seat, the front ceiling speakers should hang about 45° up in front of you (Dolby allows 30 to 55°), the rear ones just as far up behind you (125 to 150°, counted from the front). With only two ceiling speakers they hang almost above you, at about 80° (allowed: 65 to 100°).',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where do the surround speakers go?',
    a: 'With 7.1 and 9.1, beside you and slightly behind (90 to 110°, counted from the front). The rear speakers go diagonally behind you (135 to 150°). For 5.1 Dolby gives 110 to 120° for the sides, but 90 to 110° for 5.1.2 and 5.1.4 – so the planner accepts 90 to 120°.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How high should the speakers be?',
    a: 'The speakers around you should all be at the same height, roughly at seated ear height (usually about 1.2 m). The rear ones may hang a bit higher, but no more than 1.25 times as high as the front ones. Ceiling speakers should be 2 to 3 times as high as the ear-level speakers.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does this cover DTS:X?',
    a: 'The planner only checks Dolby’s rules. We haven’t looked at the DTS rules.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'Plan My Cinema – Speaker planner',
    url: 'https://plan-my-cinema.com/en/speaker-layout/',
    applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Web',
    inLanguage: 'en',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'EUR' },
    description,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
  },
];
---
<Base lang="en" page="speakers" title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>Speaker <span>layout</span></h1>
    <p>Where your speakers belong for Dolby Atmos – from 5.1 to 9.1.6 – and whether they sound right from every seat.</p>
  </div>

  <SpeakerPlanner client:load lang="en" />

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>What is this about?</h2>
    <p>
      In a home cinema the sound doesn’t just come from the front, but from everywhere: from the sides, from behind and – with
      <strong>Dolby Atmos</strong> – even from above. For that to feel real, the speakers have to be in the right places. Dolby, the company behind
      Atmos, has written down rules for this. This planner shows where your speakers belong and whether your positions follow those rules.
    </p>

    <h3>What do numbers like 7.1.4 mean?</h3>
    <p>
      The first number is how many speakers stand around you at ear height. The second is the subwoofer for the deep bass. The third is how many
      speakers hang on the ceiling. So 7.1.4 means: 7 speakers around you, 1 subwoofer and 4 on the ceiling.
    </p>

    <h3>Green, yellow, red</h3>
    <p>
      For every speaker Dolby gives an angle it should sit at – measured from your seat. <strong>Green</strong> means the speaker is in exactly the
      right place, <strong>yellow</strong> means just off, <strong>red</strong> means clearly off. To start with, the planner puts every speaker in a
      good spot itself. Use “Move” to enter where they really are in your room. The front speakers start out behind the screen – that only works with
      a screen that lets sound through. If yours doesn’t, set “Acoustically transparent screen” to “no”.
    </p>

    <h3>When several people are watching</h3>
    <p>
      Dolby’s rules are for the best seat, the <strong>main seat</strong>. From the other seats the angles look different. Enter how many seats there
      are under “Seating rows”. Then you can see for each seat how well the speakers fit there. If a sofa sits off to one side or an armchair is
      further back, you can move individual seats too.
    </p>

    <h3>Aiming the speakers</h3>
    <p>
      A speaker sounds best when it points straight at you. Sit far off to the side and you hear less of the high notes. The planner tells you how
      many degrees to turn and tilt each speaker away from the wall. It also checks whether every seat lies within the speaker’s
      “coverage”. You’ll find the dispersion angle in your speaker’s data sheet.
    </p>

    <h3>Does the sound arrive everywhere at the same time?</h3>
    <p>
      An AV receiver tunes the system for one seat: it holds back the closer speakers a tiny bit so that everything arrives at that seat at the same
      time and loudness. At the other seats that’s no longer quite right. The planner shows how many milliseconds and how much loudness it is off
      at each seat.
    </p>

    <h3>Can I see over the heads in front?</h3>
    <p>
      With several rows, the back row has to see the bottom of the picture over the heads of the row in front. The planner works out how much room is
      left between your line of sight and the head in front. About 12 centimetres is good. If it’s less, a higher platform for the back row, more space
      between the rows or a higher screen helps.
    </p>

    <h3>Where do absorbers go?</h3>
    <p>
      A speaker’s sound doesn’t just travel straight to you – it also bounces off the walls, ceiling and floor. These echoes arrive just after the
      sound itself and make it harder to tell where a noise is coming from. The planner shows you the spots where the sound bounces. That’s where
      <strong>absorbers</strong> help – panels that soak up sound.
    </p>

    <h3>How exact is this?</h3>
    <p>
      The angles come from Dolby’s official documents. The other calculations assume an empty room. How it sounds in the end also depends on your
      speakers and your room – so trust your ears too.
    </p>

    <details class="tech">
      <summary>For the technically minded: how we calculate</summary>

    <h3>Based on Dolby’s guidelines</h3>
    <p>
      All angles come from the <a href="https://www.dolby.com/siteassets/technologies/dolby-atmos/atmos-installation-guidelines-121318_r3.1.pdf" rel="noopener">Dolby Atmos Home Theater Installation Guidelines</a>{' '}
      (December 2018) and Dolby’s <a href="https://www.dolby.com/about/support/guide/speaker-setup-guides/" rel="noopener">speaker setup guides</a> (March 2020). We did not
      find a newer document.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Dolby range</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Left/Right</td><td>22–30° (guidelines fig. 3 says 22–40°)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Centre</td><td>0°</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wides (9.1.x)</td><td>50–70°</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Surround 7.1/9.1</td><td>90–110°</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Surround 5.1 / 5.1.x</td><td>110–120° / 90–110° (planner: 90–120°)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rear surround</td><td>135–150°</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Top front</td><td>elevation 45° (30–55°)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Top middle (.2 / .6)</td><td>80° (65–100°) / 90° (70–110°)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Top rear</td><td>135° (125–150°)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Dolby’s documents disagree in places: every layout drawing shows 22–30° for left/right, but figure 3 of the guidelines says 22–40°. For 5.1 the
      surrounds sit at 110–120°, for 5.1.2/5.1.4 at 90–110°. The planner shows the narrower range as green and the wider one as yellow.
    </p>

    <h3>Angles</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Azimuth</strong> is the top-view angle from the main listening position, with 0° pointing at the screen centre. <strong>Elevation</strong> is the
      side-view angle: 0° to the front, 90° straight overhead, 180° to the rear – the same convention Dolby uses.
    </p>

    <h3>Automatic placement</h3>
    <p>
      Unless you enter your own position, the planner puts each speaker at the middle of its Dolby range and moves it out to the wall (15 cm gap).
      Left, centre and right sit behind the screen, which assumes an acoustically transparent fabric. Without one, set “Acoustically transparent screen” to “no”: the planner then puts L and R at least 15 cm beyond the image edge and the centre below the screen (the 15 cm are our own choice). “Move” lets you place any speaker freely as well. Ceiling speakers go on the ceiling, as far out to the side as left/right would be at 30° – which is
      Dolby’s advice for overhead spacing. Use “Move” to enter where a speaker actually is.
    </p>

    <h3>Traffic light</h3>
    <p>
      Green means within Dolby’s range. Yellow means up to 10° outside, or 30–40° for left/right. Red is further out. The 10° margin is our own choice;
      Dolby gives no tolerance beyond its ranges.
    </p>

    <h3>Multiple seats and rows</h3>
    <p>
      Dolby refers every angle to the main listening position (MLP). With several people seated, the angles shift for the outer seats. The planner
      therefore checks each seat: pick one under “Show angles for”, and the “all seats” column shows the worst rating per speaker across every seat.
      If a sofa sits off-centre or an armchair further back, move any seat except the main one on its own (“Move seat”); its height stays that of the row.
    </p>
    <h3>Aiming (toe-in) and coverage</h3>
    <p>
      A speaker sounds best on its axis; the further off to the side you sit, the more it loses, mainly in the treble. The planner works out how many
      degrees each speaker has to be turned away from its wall and tilted so that it points at the main seat or the centre of all seats. Using the
      dispersion angle from the data sheet – per group or per speaker – it checks whether each seat lies within the speaker’s coverage. For ceiling
      speakers Dolby’s guidelines (p. 7) say: if they radiate widely (about ±45° from 100 Hz to 10 kHz) they may point straight down; aimable models
      with narrower dispersion should be angled towards the main seat. Dolby gives no toe-in angle for ear-level speakers.
    </p>
    <h3>Timing and level per seat</h3>
    <p>
      An AV receiver calibrates for one seat: it delays the closer speakers and matches levels so everything arrives at the main seat at the same
      time and loudness. At every other seat that balance is slightly off. The planner shows, for each seat, how many milliseconds each speaker
      arrives early or late and how much louder or quieter it is – worked out from the distances with the inverse-distance law, without room
      reflections.
    </p>
    <h3>Sightline</h3>
    <p>
      With several rows, the back row must see the bottom of the image over the heads in front. The planner calculates the C-value: how far the
      sightline passes above the eyes of the person in front. Commonly quoted figures are 12 cm optimal, 9 cm recommended and 6 cm minimum; we found no
      binding standard. A higher riser, more row spacing or a higher screen helps.
    </p>
    <h3>First reflections</h3>
    <p>
      Every speaker reaches the seats not only directly but also via the walls, ceiling and floor, and these early reflections blur imaging. The planner
      traces them with the image-source method for every speaker and all six room surfaces. It gives the exact point for the main seat and, with several
      seats, the patch that covers all of their points – the minimum size for an absorber. Both views draw that patch. Surfaces a speaker is mounted on
      are left out.
    </p>
    </details>

    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    {faq.map((f) => (
      <details>
        <summary>{f.q}</summary>
        <p>{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </article>
</Base>
